      "latency_ms": 2316.9,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 552,
      "output": "At its simplest, **water** is a clear, odorless, and tasteless liquid that is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nTo understand water fully, it helps to look at it from a few different perspectives:\n\n### 1. The Chemical Perspective\n* **Chemical Formula:** $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$\n* This means every single molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* Water is a **molecule** held together by covalent bonds, and because of the way the atoms are shaped, it has a slight positive charge on the hydrogen side and a slight negative charge on the oxygen side. This makes water a \"polar\" molecule, which is why it is so good at dissolving other substances (like salt and sugar).\n\n### 2. The Physical Perspective\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states:\n* **Liquid:** The standard form we drink, swim in, and rain from the sky.\n* **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water. Uniquely, solid water is *less dense* than liquid water, which is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n* **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water gas present in the air and in steam.\n\n### 3. The Ecological Perspective (Earth's Lifeblood)\n* **Coverage:** Water covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, mostly in oceans and seas (about 96.5% of Earth's water is salt water).\n* **Freshwater:** Only about 2.5% of Earth's water is fresh, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% is easily accessible for human use.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly moving in a closed loop (evaporation, condensation, precipitation, collection). The water you drink today is the same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago.\n\n### 4. The Biological Perspective\n* **Life Support:** Water is the medium in which the metabolic and cellular processes of life take place. \n* **Human Body:** The adult human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. We need it to regulate body temperature, transport nutrients, flush out waste, and keep our organs functioning. Without water, a human can usually only survive for about 3 days.\n\n**In short:** Water is the fundamental fluid of our planet—a simple chemical combination that makes life, weather, and geography possible."
